WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.220
- Some people have been
taking quarantine time

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:05.080
to bake bread and do all
this stuff that I don't do.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.110
(Mayim laughing)
But is it true

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:08.200
that you've been trying out TikTok dances?

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.570
And I really wanna see one. (laughs)

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:12.020
- Well, here's the thing.

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:14.770
I think that anyone with
children is probably observing

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:16.330
them playing a lot more video games

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:17.680
than you had planned this summer,

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.310
because of the quarantine.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:20.420
And so one of the things

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.500
that I've been trying
to do is find any way

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.390
to engage with them, or connect with them,

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:29.160
and yes, my older son
who's 14 has gotten into,

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.600
I mean, he's always on his phone,

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.750
and usually he's scrolling through TikTok,

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:34.110
which I didn't even know what it was.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.490
So I have a YouTube channel

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.440
and so we did a video
that we posted on YouTube

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.107
of me, basically, saying to my kid like,

00:00:40.107 --> 00:00:41.600
"What is TikTok?

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:42.790
How do I do it?"

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:44.263
There's a lot of this motion.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.010
They've got names for it.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:48.730
There's a lot of, like no,

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.990
and it goes with the song. (laughs)

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:52.160
- Oh my god, I love this!

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.810
I think this should be a
challenge for all parents

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:54.900
to have to do.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.690
I tried to do one that Normani,

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:57.700
I can't remember what the,

00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:58.690
it was some kind of dance challenge.

00:00:58.690 --> 00:00:59.800
There was a basketball involved.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.260
I had to pop it off my behind.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.575
I was like, look--
- They're very fast.

00:01:02.575 --> 00:01:04.230
It's like it's very fast,

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.760
and I was like, "Why can't we do it slow?"

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.210
And Miles, my older son said,
"'Cause you're an old person,

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.603
and you shouldn't be on TikTok." (laughs)

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:13.003
- (laughs) 'Cause you
shouldn't be on TikTok,

00:01:13.003 --> 00:01:13.836
that's awesome.

00:01:13.836 --> 00:01:16.980
So, I ask this only because
you're a neuroscientist

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:17.830
in real life.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:20.539
How does dancing actually help our brains,

00:01:20.539 --> 00:01:21.620
and does it?

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.825
- Oh, yes, so, I mean,

00:01:23.825 --> 00:01:27.170
any movement is good,
especially for your mood,

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:28.713
especially during quarantine.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.580
That kind of movement, it does,

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.900
it stimulates serotonin,
also doing something

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:35.860
that's enjoyable, it increases the amount

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:38.180
of happy hormones that are in your brain

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:39.650
and in your body.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:42.850
Also, things like
adrenaline are important,

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:44.770
getting your heart moving,
'cause I literally feel

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:46.310
like I've been barefoot
for three months (laughs)

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:47.550
sitting on my couch.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.250
So any time that I'm doing something

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:50.980
other than walking to the fridge,

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.890
or the bathroom, it feels
like a real accomplishment,

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:55.110
and that actually is,

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:56.260
it is, it's good for your brain.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.070
It's good for your physiology,

00:01:58.070 --> 00:01:59.880
and that's one of, kind
of, the only things,

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.860
especially in the heat of the quarantine,

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.020
it's really one of the only things

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:05.830
that we have been able to
do, is just move our bodies.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:07.160
Sometimes it's TikTok dances,

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.044
sometimes it's vigorous walking.

00:02:09.044 --> 00:02:10.660
- (laughs) Yeah, I'll take walking,

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:12.940
and music, or an audio, or Audible,

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.253
whatever, listen to a book.

